{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And when you reap the harvest of your land, etc.</b> The juxtaposition of this verse to the passage of the omer appears along the lines of the midrash (Bemidbar Rabbah 17:5) which says that the Holy One, blessed be He, surrounded Israel with commandments in every deed: if he goes out to plow — 'you shall not plow with an ox and an ass'; if he goes out to sow — 'you shall not sow diverse kinds'; if he goes out to reap — 'you shall not wholly reap the corner of your field,' etc. And one might have thought that this field, since they reap from it the omer and a commandment has already been performed with it, is no longer subject to another commandment afterward — therefore Scripture says: ״לֹא תְכַלֶּה פְּאַת שָׂדְךָ״, to tell you that in any event this commandment does not exempt you from gleanings, the forgotten sheaf, and the corner. And through this is settled the fact that it does not mention 'your vineyard,' because the reaping of the omer does not apply to the vineyard, and from where would exemption from it come? And a great proof for our words is what is cited in the Yalkut (Emor, 23:10) in the name of Torat Kohanim: 'Even once he has brought it, he begins and reaps and is obligated in gleanings, the forgotten sheaf, and the corner,' etc. — and one might have thought that the entire field is exempt; it comes to teach us otherwise." "Another matter, as is cited in the Gemara (Rosh HaShanah 16a): Rabbi Akiva said: Why did the Torah say to bring the omer at Pesach? So that the grain in the field be blessed for you; and through the two loaves the fruits of the tree are blessed; and through the water libation on the festival the rains of the year are blessed. And so that there not be in a person's heart a worthless thing, saying: since on account of the omer and the two loaves and the water libation the year's produce will be blessed, if so, why should I give gifts to the poor, 'so that YHWH your Elohim may bless me'? Therefore Scripture says: ״וּבְקֻצְרְכֶם״, etc. — for it is good that you grasp this and also from that do not release your hand, for both this and that are efficacious. But according to Rashi's explanation it is difficult: what do offerings have to do with gifts to the poor? And as it appears, there is some resemblance between them, for the offerings too are mostly eaten by the priests by way of charity. And the Ramban explained a hint to the reaping of the omer, that it does not push aside gleanings and the corner."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The Yalkut citation's final phrase appears elliptical in the Hebrew; rendered literally as 'it comes to teach us,' preserving the implied 'otherwise' that the logic demands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:11.915442089Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}